==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₁eg-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|indigence}}, late 14th century, from {{der|en|fro|indigence}} (13th century), from {{der|en|la|indigentia}}, from {{m|la|indigentem}}, form of {{m|la|indigēre||to need}}, from {{m|la|indu||in, within}} + {{m|la|egēre||be in need, want}}.<ref>{{R:Online Etymology Dictionary}}</ref>

Only relation to antonym {{m|en|affluence}} is common Latinate suffix {{suffix|en||ence}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɪndɪd͡ʒəns/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-indigence.ogg|Audio (Mid-Atlantic)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# [[extreme|Extreme]] [[poverty]] or [[destitution]].
#: {{syn|en|indigency}}
#: {{ant|en|affluence}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2002|origyear=2001|author={{w|Salman Rushdie}}|title={{w|Fury (Rushdie novel)|Fury: A Novel}}|location=London|publisher={{w|Vintage Books|Vintage}}|isbn=0099421860|page=4|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/furynovel0000rush/page/4/mode/2up?q=indigence|passage=On Professor Solanka’s street, well-heeled white youths lounged in baggy garments on roseate stoops, stylishly simulating '''indigence''' while they waited for the billionairedom that would surely be along sometime soon.}}

